# One known disagreement per line: `<id> | <shape> | <reason>`. The shape is the oracle's own label
# (printed with each DISAGREEMENT). The id names a fixed witness program in
# formal/SproutCore/Witnesses.lean; an entry whose witness no longer disagrees with its shape
# fails the run, so the list cannot rot, and an entry with no witness is an error
# (docs/lean-model-v0.md §6). Lines starting with `#` are comments.
#
# Compiler gap: spec §5.2.1 and §5.2.2 make an effectful right-hand side of `let ... in` and of a
# `do` `let` an error; the checker accepts it in an `!{IO}` function. Known and scheduled:
# docs/try-propagate-v0.md §4.5 and §9 Q15 (step 5 enforces it). The model follows the spec.
let-rhs-io | model-rejects/compiler-accepts: let-rhs-io | compiler does not enforce the pure right-hand side of let..in and do-let (spec §5.2.1, §5.2.1a)
# Spec gap: the compiler applies a syntactic value restriction to a top-level `let` (`is_syntactic_value`
# in infer.sprout, docs/fundamentals-code-review-handoff-2026-07-03.md W3) and rejects a non-value
# initialiser whose type still has free variables. spec §5.2 and §7 rule 16 say nothing about it.
value-restriction | model-accepts/compiler-rejects: value restriction | the spec does not state the value restriction on top-level let (compiler rejects non-value let with a polymorphic type)
# Compiler bug: a top-level `let` that calls a pure function value bound by an earlier non-value
# top-level `let` (so not generalised, its effect variable stays open) is rejected as "performs IO
# when its module loads". The spec calls that `let` pure, and says an unresolved effect variable is
# accepted (spec §7 rule 8, property 3). `let f = let v = 0 in \(x: Bool) -> x` then `let g = f(false)`.
nongen-call | model-accepts/compiler-rejects: top-level _ performs IO when its module loads, but top-level bindings must be pure | compiler rejects a pure top-level let that calls a non-generalised function value (open effect variable counted as IO; spec §5.2, §7 rule 8 property 3)
